  "llm_reasoning_text": "\n## Index Recommendation Reasoning\n\n### LINEITEM indexes (largest table \u2014 most impactful)\n\n1. **`lineitem(l_shipdate, l_discount, l_quantity, l_extendedprice)`** \u2014 Covers Q6 with a perfect index-only scan (cost drops from ~170K to ~35K). Also accelerates Q14 and Q15 shipdate range scans as a bitmap index.\n\n2. **`lineitem(l_orderkey, l_shipdate, l_extendedprice, l_discount)`** \u2014 Enables a nested-loop index lookup for Q3 (l_orderkey + l_shipdate filter), reducing cost from ~283K to ~189K. The leading l_orderkey column also benefits Q4 and general join probes.\n\n3. **`lineitem(l_orderkey, l_commitdate, l_receiptdate)`** \u2014 Key for Q4's EXISTS subquery. Allows index-only scan per order key checking commitdate < receiptdate, reducing cost from ~164K to ~57K. Also supports Q12's date-based filtering.\n\n4. **`lineitem(l_receiptdate, l_shipmode, l_orderkey, l_commitdate, l_shipdate)`** \u2014 Tailored for Q12 which filters on l_receiptdate range plus l_shipmode IN ('RAIL','AIR'), reducing cost from ~210K to ~76K via index-only scan.\n\n5. **`lineitem(l_orderkey, l_returnflag, l_extendedprice, l_discount)`** \u2014 Serves Q10 which joins on l_orderkey and filters l_returnflag = 'R', enabling a nested-loop index scan and reducing cost from ~175K to ~122K.\n\n6. **`lineitem(l_suppkey, l_orderkey, l_receiptdate, l_commitdate)`** \u2014 Critical for Q21 which probes lineitem by supplier, checking receiptdate > commitdate. Reduces cost from ~493K to ~255K by enabling indexed lookup on l_suppkey.\n\n7. **`lineitem(l_shipdate, l_orderkey, l_suppkey, l_extendedprice, l_discount)`** \u2014 Optimizes Q7 which filters l_shipdate BETWEEN 1995 and 1996 and joins on l_orderkey and l_suppkey, reducing cost from ~195K to ~114K with an index-only range scan.\n\n8. **`lineitem(l_partkey, l_quantity)`** \u2014 Serves Q17's correlated subquery that looks up average l_quantity by l_partkey. Dramatically reduces cost from ~1 billion to ~17K by enabling index-only scans for both the inner AVG subquery and the outer filter.\n\n9. **`lineitem(l_partkey, l_suppkey, l_shipdate, l_quantity)`** \u2014 Serves Q20's correlated subquery computing SUM(l_quantity) for specific part/supplier/shipdate combinations. Reduces per-row subquery cost from full seq scan to a narrow index-only scan (from ~186B to ~6.8B overall).\n\n10. **`lineitem(l_orderkey, l_quantity)`** \u2014 Benefits Q18's GROUP BY l_orderkey HAVING SUM(l_quantity) > 312 and the outer join back to lineitem, enabling index-only scans. Reduces cost from ~894K to ~413K.\n\n### ORDERS indexes\n\n11. **`orders(o_orderdate, o_custkey, o_orderkey, o_shippriority)`** \u2014 Covers Q3 (orderdate < date filter, join on custkey), Q5 (orderdate range), Q10 (orderdate range), and Q7/Q8 multi-joins. Enables index-only scans for the date-range access path across many queries.\n\n12. **`orders(o_orderdate, o_orderpriority, o_orderkey)`** \u2014 Specific to Q4's outer orders scan with the date range filter (1996-05-01 to 1996-08-01), enabling an index-only scan and reducing cost from ~164K to ~57K alongside the lineitem index.\n\n13. **`orders(o_custkey, o_orderkey)`** \u2014 Serves Q22's NOT EXISTS anti-join subquery (checking no orders exist per customer), reducing cost from baseline to ~13K. Also helps Q13's left join and Q21.\n\n14. **`orders(o_orderstatus, o_orderkey)`** \u2014 Serves Q21 which filters o_orderstatus = 'F' then probes by o_orderkey in a nested loop.\n\n### CUSTOMER indexes\n\n15. **`customer(c_mktsegment, c_custkey)`** \u2014 Q3 filters c_mktsegment = 'BUILDING' and returns c_custkey. Index-only scan reduces customer access cost dramatically (from seq scan ~4K to index scan ~877).\n\n### PART indexes\n\n16. **`part(p_size, p_type, p_partkey, p_mfgr)`** \u2014 Q2 filters p_size = 21 AND p_type LIKE '%STEEL', enabling index seek on p_size with p_type post-filter, plus covering p_partkey and p_mfgr.\n\n17. **`part(p_type, p_partkey)`** \u2014 Q8 filters p_type = 'MEDIUM BURNISHED STEEL' exactly, enabling an index-only scan (cost ~55) instead of a seq scan.\n\n18. **`part(p_brand, p_type, p_size, p_partkey)`** \u2014 Q16 groups by p_brand, p_type, p_size after filtering p_brand, p_type, p_size IN list. Supports sorted aggregate without extra sort.\n\n19. **`part(p_partkey, p_brand, p_container)`** \u2014 Q17 filters p_brand = 'Brand#51' AND p_container = 'LG CASE'; p_partkey as leading column also serves as a lookup key for joins in Q14/Q19.\n\n20. **`part(p_name, p_partkey)`** \u2014 Q9 filters p_name LIKE '%honeydew%' (no index benefit for leading wildcard but index-only scan still faster) and Q20 filters p_name LIKE 'burlywood%' (prefix match benefits from index scan).\n\n### PARTSUPP indexes\n\n21. **`partsupp(ps_partkey, ps_suppkey, ps_supplycost)`** \u2014 Q2's correlated subquery looks up minimum ps_supplycost per ps_partkey; covering index allows index-only scans for both the outer join and the correlated MIN subquery.\n\n22. **`partsupp(ps_suppkey, ps_partkey, ps_supplycost, ps_availqty)`** \u2014 Q11 joins partsupp to supplier (filtered by nation), then groups by ps_partkey. Index on ps_suppkey as leading column enables direct lookup of partsupp rows per supplier, reducing cost from ~52K to ~8K.\n\n### SUPPLIER indexes\n\n23. **`supplier(s_nationkey, s_suppkey)`** \u2014 Q11 looks up suppliers by nation; this index allows a nested-loop from nation to supplier to partsupp. Also benefits Q2, Q5, Q7, Q20, Q21 which all join supplier on s_nationkey.\n",
